<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Spanish Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1931 CE – 1939 CE · capital Madrid</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spain's democratic republic of the 1930s, whose sweeping reforms and deep divisions ended in civil war and Franco's dictatorship.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After republican parties won the cities in the municipal elections of 12 April 1931, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii">Alfonso XIII</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the republic was proclaimed on 14 April, ending <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/restoration-spain">Restoration Spain</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-constitution-of-1931">Constitution of December 1931</a> created a secular, democratic state with women's suffrage.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governments led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-azana">Manuel Azaña</a> pursued land reform, army reform, separation of Church and state and Catalan autonomy. A centre-right coalition reversed many reforms after 1933, and the Asturian miners' revolution of 1934 was crushed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-spain">Popular Front</a> won the elections of February 1936 amid rising political violence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A military coup on 17-18 July 1936 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>. The Republic, aided by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the International <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigades</a>, lost to Franco's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nationalist-faction-spanish-civil-war">Nationalists</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> fell in March 1939, and the war ended on 1 April, giving way to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/second-spanish-republic.jpg" alt="Second Spanish Republic" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: SanchoPanzaXXI · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Spain_(1931%E2%80%931939).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1931 CE – 1939 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Madrid</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Niceto Alcalá-Zamora, Manuel Azaña, Juan Negrín</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Catalan, Basque, Galician</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Spanish Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1935&amp;focus=second-spanish-republic" aria-label="Second Spanish Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4589 733 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Second Spanish Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4909 989L4864 1148M4909 989L4735 1360M4909 989L4655 1514M4909 989L4909 989M4909 989L4909 989M4909 989L5053 958M4909 989L4934 907M4909 989L4999 963M4909 989L4863 890"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4864 1148h0M4735 1360h0M4655 1514h0M4909 989h0M4909 989h0M5053 958h0M4934 907h0M4999 963h0M4863 890h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4909 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Madrid. <a href="/atlas?year=1935&amp;focus=second-spanish-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
